                <td style="padding: 0.85rem 1rem;"><a href="/histogram-maker/" style="color: var(--color-accent); text-decoration: underline;">Histogram maker</a> (for binning continuous observations)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         SECTION 3: WHEN TO USE / WHEN NOT TO USE
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="topical-section" id="when-to-use" style="margin-top: 4rem;">
      <div class="topical-container">
        <h2 class="section-title">When to Use a Box Plot (and When Not To)</h2>
        <p class="topical-intro">
          Knowing when to deploy a box plot ensures your findings communicate maximum insight while preventing common visualization pitfalls.
        </p>

        <div class="topical-two-col" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 2rem;">
          <!-- When to use -->
          <div class="topical-col topical-col-good" style="background: var(--color-bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 2rem;">
            <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
              <span style="color: var(--color-primary); display: flex;" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"></polyline></svg>
              </span>
              <h3 style="color: var(--color-primary); font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 0;">When a Box Plot is Ideal</h3>
            </div>
            <ul class="topical-list" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
              <li><strong>Comparing experimental treatments:</strong> Comparing control vs. drug dosage groups in clinical trials or scientific experiments.</li>
              <li><strong>Benchmarking operational metrics:</strong> Evaluating API latency, transaction durations, or page load times across multiple servers.</li>
              <li><strong>Analyzing student test scores:</strong> Comparing exam performance across 5 different classrooms or academic semesters.</li>
              <li><strong>Identifying extreme anomalies:</strong> Systematically tagging outliers using objective 1.5×IQR criteria rather than subjective visual guesses.</li>
              <li><strong>Handling large datasets:</strong> Summarizing thousands of records with zero performance lag or graphical clutter.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <!-- When not to use -->
          <div class="topical-col topical-col-limit" style="background: var(--color-bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 12px; padding: 2rem;">
            <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem;">
              <span style="color: #ff6b6b; display: flex;" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><line x1="15" y1="9" x2="9" y2="15"></line><line x1="9" y1="9" x2="15" y2="15"></line></svg>
              </span>
              <h3 style="color: #ff6b6b; font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 0;">When NOT to Use a Box Plot</h3>
            </div>
            <ul class="topical-list" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.7;">
              <li><strong>Very small sample sizes (N &lt; 20):</strong> Quartile estimates become unstable on tiny samples. For small collections, <a href="/" style="color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">use our free dot plot maker</a> or <a href="/stem-and-leaf-plot-maker/" style="color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">stem-and-leaf plot maker</a> so every individual observation remains visible.</li>
              <li><strong>Multimodal distributions:</strong> A bimodal dataset (with two distinct clusters) produces a box plot that looks identical to a uniform dataset. To view peaks and valleys, <a href="/histogram-maker/" style="color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">generate a histogram</a> instead.</li>
              <li><strong>Purely categorical data:</strong> When comparing categorical totals or group counts, deploy a bar chart or <a href="/categorical-scatter-plot/" style="color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">categorical scatter plot</a>.</li>
              <li><strong>In-depth outlier diagnostics:</strong> When investigating the exact impact and root causes of anomalies, consult our <a href="/blog/outlier-dot-plot-maker/" style="color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">outlier detection guide</a>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         SECTION 4: HOW TO READ A BOX PLOT
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="topical-section" id="how-to-read" style="margin-top: 4rem;">
      <div class="topical-container">
        <h2 class="section-title">How to Read a Box Plot: Skewness, Spread, and Group Differences</h2>
        <p class="topical-intro">
          Reading a box plot takes only moments once you understand how to decode the four core visual signatures: center position, box span, whisker asymmetry, and isolated dots.
        </p>

        <div class="topical-grid topical-grid-4" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <!-- Signature 1: Skewness -->
          <div class="topical-card topical-card-accent">
            <div class="topical-card-badge" style="color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Asymmetry</div>
            <h3>Reading Skewness</h3>
            <p style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
              When the median line is closer to Q1 and the upper whisker is elongated, the distribution is <strong>right-skewed</strong> (e.g. executive compensation). When the median sits closer to Q3 with a long lower whisker, it is <strong>left-skewed</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Signature 2: Spread & Variability -->
          <div class="topical-card topical-card-purple">
            <div class="topical-card-badge" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Dispersion</div>
            <h3>Evaluating Spread</h3>
            <p style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
              A long box signals high variability and a large interquartile range across the core observations. A squashed box indicates tight clustering around the center. Compare whisker lengths to assess total range variability.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Signature 3: Comparing Groups -->
          <div class="topical-card topical-card-orange">
            <div class="topical-card-badge" style="color: var(--color-warning); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Parallel Comparison</div>
            <h3>Comparing Groups</h3>
            <p style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
              Check whether median lines are aligned or shifted. Look at box overlap: if two boxes do not overlap along the numerical scale, the groups likely exhibit statistically significant differences. Enable notches to view 95% confidence intervals.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Signature 4: Spotting Outliers -->
          <div class="topical-card topical-card-red">
            <div class="topical-card-badge" style="color: var(--color-error); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Anomalies</div>
            <h3>Spotting Outliers</h3>
            <p style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
              Points plotted beyond whisker caps are verified statistical outliers. Notice whether outliers appear exclusively on one end (indicating a heavy one-sided tail) or symmetrically on both extremes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
