      <div class="topical-container">
        <h2 class="section-title">Box Plots in Statistics Education & Curriculums</h2>
        <p class="topical-intro">
          From sixth-grade common core classrooms to AP Statistics exams and college data science sequences, box plots are fundamental to teaching non-parametric summary statistics and exploratory data analysis.
        </p>

        <div class="topical-grid topical-grid-3" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
          <!-- Level 1: Common Core 6.SP -->
          <div class="topical-card">
            <div class="topical-card-badge" style="color: var(--color-primary); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Middle School</div>
            <h3>Common Core 6.SP Standards</h3>
            <p style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
              Standard <strong>CCSS.MATH.CONTENT.6.SP.B.4</strong> requires students to display numerical data in plots on a number line, including dot plots, histograms, and box plots. Standard <strong>6.SP.B.5.C</strong> emphasizes reporting measures of center (median) and variability (interquartile range). Our tool enables students to test concepts without spreadsheet setup.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Level 2: AP Statistics -->
          <div class="topical-card">
            <div class="topical-card-badge" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Advanced Placement</div>
            <h3>AP Statistics Curriculum</h3>
            <p style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
              Unit 1 of the AP Statistics course requires students to describe one-variable distributions using the <strong>SOCS</strong> framework (Shape, Outliers, Center, and Spread). Free-response questions frequently ask students to compare parallel box plots and justify whether extreme values meet the <code>1.5 × IQR</code> outlier rule.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Level 3: College Stats & Data Science -->
          <div class="topical-card">
            <div class="topical-card-badge" style="color: #4d96ff; font-weight: 700; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 0.75rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Higher Education</div>
            <h3>College Stats & Data Science</h3>
            <p style="color: var(--color-text-secondary); line-height: 1.6;">
              In university biostatistics, engineering, and data science courses, box plots serve as the primary visual diagnostic before running ANOVA tests, checking variance homogeneity (homoscedasticity), and conducting robust regression. Notched box plots provide instant visual inference tests for median equality.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

